(docs/BUGS_FOUND.md #130: what counts as a number, read at run time. A
 text is a number when the whole of it could be written as a number
 literal in Vox source, after one optional minus: digits, where a leading
 zero means nothing, with an optional fractional part, or a whole number
 after 0x, 0b or 0o. Each of these is a number, so the flag stays down
 and the value is stored. As a number, a fractional part is dropped, as
 it is when a float is cast to a number. A number too large to hold is
 not one, and keeps the old value.)

a number called count is 1.
a float called ratio is 1.0.
a text called sample is "-7".
Set count to sample as a number.
Print count.
Set sample to "0234".
Set count to sample as a number.
Print count.
Set sample to "007".
Set count to sample as a number.
Print count.
Set sample to "-0".
Set count to sample as a number.
Print count.
Set sample to "4.8".
Set count to sample as a number.
Print count.
Set ratio to sample as a float.
Print ratio.
Set sample to "-2.5".
Set count to sample as a number.
Print count.
Set ratio to sample as a float.
Print ratio.
Set sample to "-0x345A".
Set count to sample as a number.
Print count.
Set sample to "0o234".
Set count to sample as a number.
Print count.
Set sample to "0b101".
Set count to sample as a number.
Print count.
Set sample to "0x10".
Set ratio to sample as a float.
Print ratio.
On error print "never printed".
Set sample to "9223372036854775808".
Set count to sample as a number.
On error print "too large, kept {count}".

(A text literal is read by the compiler itself, by the same rule, and
 the same spellings are number literals in the source: 0o writes octal.)

a number called padded is "0234" as a number.
a number called signed is "-0x345A" as a number.
a number called truncated is "4.8" as a number.
a float called sixteen is "0x10" as a float.
a number called prefixed is "0x1F" as a hex number.
a number called octal is 0o234.
a number called fifteen is 0O17.
Print "{padded} {signed} {truncated} {sixteen} {prefixed} {octal} {fifteen}".
